[{"id":"30721","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which year was Prithviraj III defeated by Sultan Muhammad Ghori?</span></p>","option_1":"1190","option_2":"1192","option_3":"1194","option_4":"1196","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 1192.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Prithviraj III, the Chauhan ruler of Delhi and Ajmer, was defeated by Sultan Muhammad Ghori in the Second Battle of Tarain in 1192 — a decisive turning point that paved the way for Muslim rule in northern India and led eventually to the establishment of the Delhi Sultanate. Notably, in the preceding year (1191), Prithviraj had defeated Ghori in the First Battle of Tarain.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30722","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>According to the Industrial Policy Resolution, 1948, the industries were divided into how many categories?</span></p>","option_1":"8","option_2":"4","option_3":"6","option_4":"2","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 4.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Industrial Policy Resolution of 1948 classified industries into four categories — ranging from those reserved exclusively for state monopoly, to new undertakings under state regulation, industries with potential future state control, and industries left to the private/cooperative sector — laying the foundation for a mixed economy in newly independent India.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30723","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Baba Guru Nanak emphasized on which of the following?</span></p>","option_1":"Worship of multiple gods","option_2":"Worship of one God","option_3":"Attainment of material wealth","option_4":"Worship of idols","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Worship of one God.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Guru Nanak, the founder of Sikhism, emphasised monotheism — the worship of one formless God (Ik Onkar) — rejecting idol worship and empty ritualism, while stressing equality, honest living, and sincere devotion.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30724","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>On 24th January _____, a 100% public sector enterprise, Bharat Refineries Limited, acquired complete ownership of Burmah Shell's interests in India.</span></p>","option_1":"1984","option_2":"1976","option_3":"1978","option_4":"1980","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 1976.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>On 24 January 1976, Bharat Refineries Limited (later renamed Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited, BPCL), a wholly state-owned enterprise, acquired complete ownership of Burmah Shell's assets in India, as part of the nationalisation of foreign oil companies operating in the country.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30725","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which territory was added to the Magadhan empire by Mahapadma Nanda?</span></p>","option_1":"Kaveripattinam","option_2":"Kalinga","option_3":"Taxila","option_4":"Ujjain","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Kalinga.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Mahapadma Nanda, founder of the Nanda dynasty, significantly expanded the Magadhan empire by annexing Kalinga (present-day coastal Odisha) — one of the earliest recorded conquests of the region, long before its subsequent, much better-known reconquest by Emperor Ashoka.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30726","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which part of the digestive system absorbs the most water from unabsorbed food in humans?</span></p>","option_1":"Stomach","option_2":"Liver","option_3":"Small intestine","option_4":"Large intestine","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Large intestine.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The large intestine (colon) is chiefly responsible for absorbing the bulk of the remaining water and electrolytes from undigested food matter, compacting the waste into stool before it is excreted from the body.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30727","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>According to Jain philosophy, what is the main aim of the 'Syadvada' system?</span></p>","option_1":"Promote ritual sacrifices","option_2":"Emphasize strict fundamentalism","option_3":"Deny all forms of reality","option_4":"Incorporate truths from multiple viewpoints","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Incorporate truths from multiple viewpoints.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Syadvada,' the Jain doctrine of conditioned predication (often summarised as \"maybe/in some respect\"), asserts that reality is complex and multi-faceted, and that truth can only be expressed relative to a particular standpoint — closely linked to the Jain principle of anekantavada, encouraging the acceptance of multiple valid viewpoints rather than absolute claims.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30728","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following condition can result from a deficiency of growth hormone in childhood in humans?</span></p>","option_1":"Goitre","option_2":"Dwarfism","option_3":"Hyperthyroidism","option_4":"Scurvy","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Dwarfism.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A deficiency of growth hormone (secreted by the pituitary gland) during childhood results in dwarfism, characterised by significantly stunted physical growth. Goitre results from iodine deficiency, hyperthyroidism from excess thyroid hormone, and scurvy from vitamin C deficiency — none related to growth hormone.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30729","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The 'Dosmoche' festival is celebrated in which state/UT of India?</span></p>","option_1":"Kerala","option_2":"Ladakh","option_3":"Tamil Nadu","option_4":"Goa","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Ladakh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Dosmoche,' also known as the \"Festival of the Scapegoat,\" is a Buddhist monastic festival celebrated in Ladakh, featuring masked dances and rituals performed to drive away evil spirits and negative influences at the start of the new year.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30730","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The English Education Act of 1835 was passed based on the recommendations of which of the following individuals' minute?</span></p>","option_1":"Thomas Babington Macaulay","option_2":"James Mill","option_3":"Henry Thomas Colebrooke","option_4":"William Jones","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Thomas Babington Macaulay.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The English Education Act of 1835 was passed based on the recommendations in Thomas Babington Macaulay's famous \"Minute on Indian Education\" (1835), which advocated for English-medium education in India, aiming to create a class of Indians \"Indian in blood and colour, but English in taste, in opinions, in morals, and in intellect.\"</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30731","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the SI unit of work?</span></p>","option_1":"weber","option_2":"tesla","option_3":"coulomb","option_4":"joule","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - joule.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The SI unit of work (and energy) is the joule (J), named after physicist James Prescott Joule, defined as the work done when a force of one newton displaces an object by one metre in the direction of that force.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30732","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which Indian state are 'Chhopati' folk songs traditionally performed?</span></p>","option_1":"Manipur","option_2":"Jharkhand","option_3":"Uttarakhand","option_4":"West Bengal","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Uttarakhand.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Chhopati' folk songs are traditionally performed in the state of Uttarakhand, reflecting the region's rich Garhwali and Kumaoni cultural heritage, often sung on social and agricultural occasions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30733","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who called the 'untouchables' Harijans or children of God?</span></p>","option_1":"Baba Amte","option_2":"B.R. Ambedkar","option_3":"Mahatma Gandhi","option_4":"Kandukuri Veeresalingam","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Mahatma Gandhi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Mahatma Gandhi popularised the term \"Harijan,\" meaning \"children of God,\" to refer to the so-called \"untouchables,\" as part of his campaign against untouchability and for the upliftment of the depressed classes — though the term was later criticised by many, including Dr. Ambedkar, and has largely been replaced by \"Dalit\" in contemporary usage.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30734","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which company developed the generative AI model named Muse in February 2025?</span></p>","option_1":"Sony","option_2":"Google","option_3":"Microsoft","option_4":"Nintendo","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Microsoft.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In February 2025, Microsoft unveiled \"Muse,\" a generative AI model designed for creating and simulating video game environments and gameplay, marking a notable advancement in the application of AI to game development — a current-affairs-based question.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30735","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In the late 1980s economic crisis, the international agencies impose conditions on India to get the loan for tackling the economic crisis, which of the following was NOT included in that conditions?</span></p>","option_1":"To liberalise and open the economy by removing restrictions on the private sector.","option_2":"To reduce the role of the government in many areas.","option_3":"To remove trade restrictions between India and other countries.","option_4":"To open new public enterprises in consumer goods sector.","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - To open new public enterprises in consumer goods sector..</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As part of the structural adjustment programme tied to loans from international agencies (like the IMF) during India's balance-of-payments crisis, India was required to liberalise its economy, reduce government intervention, and remove trade restrictions — a shift away from state control. Opening new public sector enterprises in the consumer goods sector directly contradicts this liberalisation-oriented direction, making it the condition that was NOT included.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30736","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which IIT developed a method using bacterial enzymes to degrade plasticizers in January 2025?</span></p>","option_1":"IIT Delhi","option_2":"IIT Madras","option_3":"IIT Roorkee","option_4":"IIT Guwahati","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - IIT Roorkee.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In January 2025, researchers at IIT Roorkee developed a method using bacterial enzymes to degrade plasticizers (chemical additives used to increase plastic flexibility), contributing to more sustainable approaches for managing plastic waste and pollution.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30737","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is an example of Plantation crop?</span></p>","option_1":"Sugarcane","option_2":"Rice","option_3":"Wheat","option_4":"Jowar","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Sugarcane.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Sugarcane is a classic example of a plantation crop — grown on large estates, requiring significant capital investment, labour, and processing infrastructure. Rice, wheat, and jowar are food/cereal crops and are not typically classified as plantation crops.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30738","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The nominal gross domestic product (GDP) is calculated at the______.</span></p>","option_1":"constant set of prices","option_2":"current prevailing prices","option_3":"prices fixed by the Reserve Bank of India","option_4":"prices fixed by the World Bank","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - current prevailing prices.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Nominal GDP is calculated using current prevailing market prices for goods and services produced in a given period, without adjusting for inflation. This is in contrast to real GDP, which uses constant (base-year) prices to account for changes in the general price level over time.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30739","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following statement is INCORRECT regarding Foreign exchange rate?</span></p>","option_1":"It is the price of one currency in terms of another.","option_2":"It enables comparison of international costs and prices.","option_3":"People demand foreign exchange because they want to purchase goods and services from other countries.","option_4":"A rise in the price of foreign exchange decreases the cost of purchasing a foreign good.","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - A rise in the price of foreign exchange decreases the cost of purchasing a foreign good..</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A rise in the price of foreign exchange (i.e., domestic currency depreciation) makes foreign currency more expensive to acquire, thereby INCREASING — not decreasing — the cost of purchasing foreign goods for domestic buyers. Hence, this statement is factually incorrect, while the other statements correctly describe the fundamental nature and role of exchange rates.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30740","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is the constraint in available surface water utilisation in India?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Topographical challenge</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Hydrological challenge</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>India's utilisation of available surface water faces both topographical challenges (uneven and difficult terrain making water transport and storage complex) and hydrological challenges (uneven seasonal and regional distribution of rainfall and river flow), making both statements correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30741","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who is the author of the book 'The Village'?</span></p>","option_1":"Mulk Raj Anand","option_2":"R. K. Narayan","option_3":"Sudeep Nagarkar","option_4":"Prayaag Akbar","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Mulk Raj Anand.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'The Village' is a novel by Mulk Raj Anand, one of the pioneers of Indo-Anglian fiction, well known for his works portraying the lives of India's poor and marginalised communities, including his acclaimed novels \"Untouchable\" and \"Coolie.\"</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30742","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which year did the GDP of India cross the 2 trillion-dollar mark?</span></p>","option_1":"2012","option_2":"2014","option_3":"2016","option_4":"2018","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 2014.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>India's GDP crossed the $2 trillion mark in 2014, reflecting the country's sustained economic growth over the preceding decades and its continued emergence as one of the world's largest economies.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30743","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Ranakpur Temple in Rajasthan sits graciously poised on the banks of River______.</span></p>","option_1":"Krishna","option_2":"Maghai","option_3":"Yamuna","option_4":"Mahanadi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Maghai.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Ranakpur Jain Temple in Rajasthan, renowned for its intricate marble carvings and architecture, is situated on the banks of the Maghai River, nestled amid the Aravalli hills in the Pali district.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30744","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is an example of micronutrient needed by the crops from the soil?</span></p>","option_1":"Nitrogen","option_2":"Iron","option_3":"Magnesium","option_4":"Sulphur","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Iron.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Iron is a micronutrient required by crops in small quantities for essential physiological processes such as chlorophyll synthesis, whereas nitrogen, magnesium, and sulphur are classified as macronutrients, needed by plants in comparatively much larger quantities.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30745","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"818","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In structure dhrupad has _____ parts and the dhrupad proper is a song in _____ parts.</span></p>","option_1":"2, 4","option_2":"5, 4","option_3":"6, 8","option_4":"8, 8","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 2, 4.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Dhrupad, one of the oldest forms of Hindustani classical vocal music, is structured broadly in two main parts, while the \"dhrupad proper\" (the composed song itself) is traditionally organised into four sections — Sthayi, Antara, Sanchari, and Abhog — reflecting the genre's disciplined and elaborate compositional structure.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]